The copy constructor is called when Text copy = message is executed. The a + b + c expression returns temporary Text objects; in C++17, guaranteed copy elision means these prvalue results do not require extra copy construction in this implementation.

Small code improvement for guaranteed copy counting: To make the a + b + c copy-count result reliably zero under C++17, replace the operator+ implementation above with the following version and add the private ownership constructor.

Add this inside the class's private section: Text(char* ownedData, bool) : data(ownedData) {}

Replace operator+ with: Text operator+(const Text& other) const { size_t len1 = strlen(data); size_t len2 = strlen(other.data);

char* combined = new char[len1 + len2 + 1];

strcpy(combined, data);
strcat(combined, other.data);

return Text(combined, true);
}

This transfers ownership of the newly allocated array to the returned object, avoiding an extra copy. 5. Design and Analysis A. Operators that cannot be overloaded Operator Meaning :: Scope resolution . Member access .* Pointer-to-member access ?: Conditional operator sizeof Size calculation

These operators cannot be overloaded because their behavior is part of the fundamental C++ language syntax. B. Rules of operator overloading

Operator precedence cannot be changed.
Operator associativity cannot be changed.
The number of operands (arity) cannot be changed.
At least one operand must be a user-defined type.
New operators cannot be created. C. Why must operator= be a member function? The assignment operator must be implemented as a member function because C++ requires overloaded operator= to be a non-static member of its class. D. When should an operator be a friend? A friend function is useful when an operator needs access to private class data but should not be a member function.
For example, operator<< is implemented as a friend because the left operand is cout, an ostream object, not a Text object. E. Self-assignment protection if (this != &other) { // Perform assignment }

This condition prevents unnecessary memory deletion and copying when an object is assigned to itself. 6. Memory Management The Text class uses dynamic memory allocation.

Before copying: Object a | v "Hello"

After deep copying: Object a Object b | | v v "Hello" "Hello"

Separate memory Separate memory

Both objects have independent memory. Changing or destroying one object does not affect the other. 7. Experimental Investigation Experiment 1: Count copies in a + b + c The program resets the copy counter and evaluates: Text::resetCopyCount(); Text combined = a + b + c;

