The benchmark compares repeated concatenation of two fixed short strings. It does not measure the cost of continually growing one long string. 8. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis The assignment suggests two alternatives.

Alternative 1: Use std::string

Instead of creating a custom Text class, the program can use the built-in string class. #include #include using namespace std;

int main() { string a = "Hello "; string b = "World"; string c = "!";

string result = a + b + c;

cout << result << endl;

return 0;
}

Output: Hello World!

std::string automatically manages memory and provides built-in concatenation, assignment and comparison.

Alternative 2: Implement + using += on a copy Text operator+(Text a, const Text& b) { a += b; return a; }

Here, the left operand is copied into a, and the second string is appended to it. This requires an appropriate operator+= implementation. Complexity comparison Let:

(n_1) = length of first string.
(n_2) = length of second string.
(n_3) = length of third string. Operation Time complexity Copy constructor O(n) Copy assignment O(n) Comparison O(min(n1, n2)) Concatenation O(n1 + n2) Printing O(n)
For a + b + c, the first concatenation copies approximately (n_1+n_2) characters. The second copies approximately (n_1+n_2+n_3) characters.

Total character-copying work is: [ O(2n_1+2n_2+n_3) ]

This is linear in the total input and output length. Temporary storage is also required for the intermediate and final strings.

std::string has the same general complexity for concatenation, but it provides optimized memory management and avoids the need to manually implement copying and destruction. 9. Conclusion The Text class successfully demonstrates dynamic memory management and operator overloading. The copy constructor creates independent copies, the assignment operator prevents self-assignment problems, and the overloaded operators make concatenation, comparison and printing easier to understand.

Although a custom Text class provides control over memory management, std::string is generally preferred in practical applications because it handles memory safely and provides ready-made operations.
